            "Ghana still loses roughly 260 to 310 women for every 100,000 live births and 17 newborns for every 1,000, and the newborn deaths are now the majority of child deaths under five. The <strong>direct causes</strong> of maternal death are the same five everywhere — haemorrhage, hypertensive disorders, sepsis, unsafe abortion and obstructed labour — with <strong>indirect causes</strong> that the community nurse sees first: anaemia, malaria, HIV, sickle cell disease and heart disease made worse by pregnancy. Newborns die of prematurity and low birth weight, of asphyxia at birth, and of infection — sepsis, pneumonia and tetanus from the cord. Behind almost every death are the <strong>three delays</strong>: the delay in <strong>deciding</strong> to seek care, because the danger sign was not recognised, the husband or mother-in-law was not home to give permission, there was no money, or the healer was tried first; the delay in <strong>reaching</strong> care, because there was no vehicle, the road was cut by the rains, or the river had no boat at night; and the delay in <strong>receiving</strong> adequate care at the facility, because there was no midwife, no blood, no oxytocin, no theatre, or no one who acted. Community nursing works on the first two delays, and the maternal death audit works on the third.",
