            "The reasons children are wrongly sent away are not contraindications: a mild fever, cough or cold; mild diarrhoea; malnutrition (vaccinate — the malnourished child needs it most); being breastfed; a family history of fits; antibiotics; prematurity or low birth weight (vaccinate at the chronological age); and a missed appointment — <strong>never restart a course</strong>; give the missed dose and continue the intervals from there. True contraindications are few: anaphylaxis to a previous dose or a component; live vaccines (BCG, OPV, MR, yellow fever) in a child with symptomatic HIV or another severe immunodeficiency; encephalopathy within seven days of a previous pertussis-containing dose; and a child sick enough to need admission, who is vaccinated on discharge. A child with HIV who is well receives all vaccines. The <strong>open-vial policy</strong>: multi-dose vials of OPV, pentavalent, Td, hepatitis B and IPV, once opened, may be used in later sessions for up to 28 days provided the expiry date has not passed, the vaccine vial monitor is usable, the vials were kept at 2–8 °C, the septum was not submerged in water and aseptic technique was used; reconstituted BCG, measles–rubella and yellow fever are discarded six hours after reconstitution or at the end of the session, whichever comes first. The right diluent, from the same manufacturer, kept cold: reconstituting with anything else — including a vial of muscle relaxant or insulin stored in the same fridge — has killed children.",
            "<strong>Adverse events following immunisation</strong> are mostly minor and expected — fever, soreness, a small nodule, the BCG papule that ulcerates and heals over weeks, the measles vaccine fever and faint rash around a week later — and the mother is told what to expect and given paracetamol advice. Serious events — anaphylaxis within minutes (adrenaline 1 in 1,000, 0.01 mL/kg intramuscularly in the anterolateral thigh, repeated at five minutes if needed, and referral), persistent screaming, convulsions, hypotonic episodes, BCG abscess or lymphadenitis, an abscess from an unsterile injection, toxic shock from a contaminated or wrongly reconstituted vial, and any death or hospitalisation or cluster — are treated, reported to the district within 24 hours on the AEFI form, and investigated, with the vial and batch kept. The child is observed for at least 15 minutes after every injection, the emergency tray with adrenaline is at every session including outreach, and the mother is told honestly what happened. Programme errors — wrong site, wrong route, wrong diluent, a frozen vaccine, a reused needle — are the preventable adverse events, and supervision is their prevention.",
