            "Adolescents aged 10 to 19 are about a fifth of Ghana's population, and their health problems are mostly behavioural and social: <strong>15 per cent of girls aged 15 to 19 have begun childbearing</strong>; about one woman in five was married before 18; sexually transmitted infections and HIV, unsafe abortion, sexual coercion and violence, alcohol, tobacco, cannabis and tramadol, road injuries, depression and suicide, anaemia and increasingly obesity, menstrual problems and the lost school days they cause. The national adolescent health policy directs that services be provided to adolescents <strong>confidentially and without requiring the consent of a parent or partner</strong>, in <strong>adolescent-friendly</strong> settings: hours that do not clash with school, a separate space or a separate hour, staff trained not to comment on the adolescent's situation, no demand for marriage, a child or a partner before contraception is offered, free or cheap services, and peer educators and youth clubs that bring young people in. A judgemental face ends the consultation and sends the girl to the chemical seller for misoprostol.",
